#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "511feb42-d0dd-55b6-85c5-f0605fcf8da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9454" :question/problem "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{e}^{t-4}) \\\\  & =2t-{e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4 \\, \\textrm{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =2(4)-{e}^{4-4} \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F| = |21| = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "3ecb82f0-3877-5732-851d-adec73abb890" :question/math-academy-id "e-9859" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-6}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-6}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t-6}+2t) \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t-6}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot (9{e}^{3t-6}+2) \\\\  & =18{e}^{3t-6}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/id #uuid "2666ce7a-356a-574a-88fd-97417afc8ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-9860" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}-30t+5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-24t-30 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t \\geq 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b58342de-c454-5afe-9ecb-ccafd98db48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149059} {:answer/id #uuid "d43ff081-53ff-551e-9f2a-b0dc4ac8ae7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149060} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} {:answer/id #uuid "1eda97be-7632-50f1-89a9-15ea96362e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149062} {:answer/id #uuid "7484898e-4401-56e2-b0e3-591df12b1c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 6s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} :answer-field/id #uuid "977bf223-9796-5239-a7a2-1373f812b958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396d3531-2c0c-5e02-bb04-58dd2fc7f372" :question/math-academy-id "q-138769" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-6t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5048da6-f06e-54ef-99f3-02d35784d53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 7s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149065} {:answer/id #uuid "f141134b-33f4-5535-a223-d62b5f9b2448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149066} {:answer/id #uuid "06ce788d-868d-5cf0-b75c-d08a7ee4d31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 4s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149067} {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} {:answer/id #uuid "60a06afb-6930-5bb6-9a10-096b29ce56c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7066d7-ace6-51fb-8248-23eb546ef0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8295ff34-59d6-595e-a3a0-97b1c540771c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138770" :question/problem "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-24t+1) \\\\  & =12t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-24 & =0 \\\\ 12 & =24 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878beeac-d61b-5bf0-9a2b-6b41aff66d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{e}^{2t}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310664} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5325b3-7241-537f-a326-f60477e4a478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2t}-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310665} {:answer/id #uuid "184eaca3-31bb-5561-8cf6-c0f17fcc7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-18t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310666} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbbd2ae-3131-53d6-a6ef-b221f80640ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310667} {:answer/id #uuid "78658f23-26dd-5b83-a1bc-29519a54624a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{e}^{2t}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "184eaca3-31bb-5561-8cf6-c0f17fcc7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-18t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310666} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e3b8fc4-2210-5501-96fa-fe95a42ffe3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce8fb41e-d014-5085-a244-2a0981843cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138901" :question/problem "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e48301-3781-5b1f-ac80-f45f4f63437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e48301-3781-5b1f-ac80-f45f4f63437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310716} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5b299de-f350-54a7-bf19-2252c4ffc75d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "485f113e-1072-57f0-bd8c-02699b28f536" :question/math-academy-id "q-345339" :question/problem "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fefe3b53-f05d-512a-a0fe-9b2334b8e141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359117} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeaa308-cd0e-5644-b253-9b3a050dda88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aeaa308-cd0e-5644-b253-9b3a050dda88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359118} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ae1eba-b1a1-511e-ba2d-0316cadded79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df1e6f0b-3cb8-5b84-956f-6e1aa296a8a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345340" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
